  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🏰 Breton Name Generator",
      "tagline": "French-style names for the Bretons of High Rock, from farmhands to knights and nobles."
    },
    "intro": "Bretons have the most French-sounding names in Tamriel: in Skyrim you meet Sybille Stentor, the court wizard in Solitude, and Amaund Motierre, the man who hires the Dark Brotherhood to kill the Emperor. This **Breton name generator** gives you new names in that style, a first name and surname, a noble's Lord or Lady, or a knight's Sir or Dame, for Skyrim, Elder Scrolls Online or your own story. It's a fan-made, unofficial tool, not connected to Bethesda Softworks or ZeniMax, and it never gives you the name of a character from the games.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How Breton names work",
        "content": "<p>Most Bretons in the later games have a first name and a surname, both French in flavour: <strong>Sybille Stentor</strong> and <strong>Amaund Motierre</strong> in Skyrim are typical. Plenty go by one name too, like <strong>Belethor</strong>, who runs the general goods shop in Whiterun, and <strong>Delphine</strong> of the Blades.</p><ul><li><strong>First names</strong> are often real <a href=\"french-name-generator.html\">French names</a> or near them: old ones like Mahaut and Archambault sit next to modern ones.</li><li><strong>Surnames</strong> sound like French family names, often built from a place or a thing: <em>Boisclair</em>, <em>Noirmont</em>, <em>Mauvoisin</em>.</li><li><strong>Titles</strong> matter in High Rock. Elder Scrolls Online is full of Breton knights called Sir and Dame, and nobles called Lord and Lady.</li></ul><p>It wasn't always this way. In Arena and Daggerfall, Breton surnames were English-sounding, built from parts like <em>-ford</em> and <em>-smith</em>. The French style came in with the later games, and it's the one this generator follows.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Eight good Breton names",
